course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 9
title: To a hundred, first and last, and the first colour
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ninety is another odd one: not «nine-ty» but devyanOsto, and then comes sto, a hundred — the big number the seeker counts to when everyone needs time to hide.
explain: Before a race someone shouts «raz, dva, try — pishLY!», one, two, three — go!, and after the counting in hide-and-seek the seeker calls ya shukAyu!, ready or not, I'm coming.
explain: Racing to the swings, kids shout ya PERshyi! (I'm first!) and ostANniy — loh! (last one's a loser!) — all in fun.
explain: Fingers help too: many Ukrainians count by bending fingers into the palm one by one, so a friend's «three» on the hand can look different from yours.
explain: And the first colour: chervOnyi, red — the colour of a football shirt, a fire engine or a ripe tomato.
fact: devyanOsto means ninety; it is an odd one, not built as «nine» plus «-ty».
fact: sto means a hundred, and na vsi sto means a hundred per cent.
fact: raz, dva, try — pishLY! means one, two, three — go!, used to start a race; pishLY means «let's go» or «we went».
fact: ya shukAyu! means I'm looking, said by the seeker in hide-and-seek, khovAnky, after counting — like «ready or not, I'm coming!».
fact: skIlky? means how many? or how much?, for counting things and asking prices.
fact: na PALtsyakh means on the fingers; many Ukrainians count by bending fingers into the palm one by one, so the hand may look different from English counting.
fact: ODyn-nol means one–nil, a football score; nol means zero or nil, and nul is also heard.
fact: PERshyi means first: ya PERshyi! is I'm first!; a girl says PERsha.
fact: ostANniy means the last one; ostANniy — loh! (last one's a loser) is said in fun when racing, and nobody minds.
fact: POlovyna means half, and navpIl means in half: dIlymo navpIl, let's split it in half.
fact: bIlshe means more and mEnshe means less.
fact: chervOnyi means red; krAsnyi is the Russian or mixed-speech word heard in some families, and in Ukrainian krasNyi means beautiful.
exercise: What does devyanOsto mean? | Ninety | Nineteen | Nine | Nine hundred
exercise: What does sto mean? | A hundred | Stop, wait a bit | Seven | Ten
exercise: What does na vsi sto mean? | A hundred per cent | Everybody stop | All at once | A hundred metres
exercise: What does raz, dva, try — pishLY! mean? | One, two, three — go! | One, two, three — stop! | One, two, three — hide! | One, two, three — sit!
exercise: When does the seeker shout ya shukAyu!? | After counting in hide-and-seek | When he is caught by the others in tag | When he is hungry | When he wins a race
exercise: What does skIlky? mean? | How many? or how much? | How old? | How far is it from here? | When?
exercise: What does na PALtsyakh mean? | On the fingers | On the palms of your feet | In the pocket | On the paper
exercise: Why might a Ukrainian friend's finger-counting look different from yours? | Many bend fingers into the palm one by one | They count with only one finger | They count backwards from ten | They use their toes too
exercise: What score is ODyn-nol? | One–nil | One–one, a draw | Zero–one to them | Eleven goals
exercise: What does nol mean? | Zero, nil | No | Nine, after eight | Full up
exercise: What does a boy shout to say «I'm first!»? | ya PERshyi! | ya PERsha! | ya ostANniy! | ya shukAyu!
exercise: What does ostANniy mean? | The last one | The first one | The best one of all | The fastest one
exercise: How do kids take «ostANniy — loh!»? | As fun — nobody minds | As a serious insult | As a rule broken | As a reason to stop playing
exercise: What does POlovyna mean? | Half | Floor | Twice | Quarter
exercise: What do bIlshe and mEnshe mean? | More and less | Big and small | First and last | Left and right
exercise: What does chervOnyi mean? | Red | Beautiful | Black | Orange
exercise: In Ukrainian, what does krasNyi mean? | Beautiful | Red | Brown | Dirty
test: «krasnyi means red, my mate's nan says it» Which is right? | krAsnyi for red is a Russian or mixed word; in Ukrainian red is chervOnyi | Yes, it is the Ukrainian red | No, it means green | No, it means hundred
test: «ninety = devyatdesyat, easy» Which is right? | No, ninety is the odd word devyanOsto | Yes, devyatdesyat is right, like nine and ten together | No, ninety is sto | No, ninety is DEvyat
test: You and Sashko split a chocolate bar. He says «POlovyna tobI». What do you get? | Half | All of it | A quarter | None of it
test: «I won ODyn-nol, so I scored one and you scored one» Which is right? | No, one–nil: one goal to zero | Yes, one each, a draw | No, it's eleven–zero, a big win | No, it's zero–one
test: Sashko's little brother shows three fingers by bending three into the palm and leaving two up. What would happen if you read the fingers still up? | You'd read two, though he means three | You'd read three correctly | You'd read five | You'd read zero
test: «na vsi sto» says Sashko about his new bike. What does he mean? | He is a hundred per cent sure or happy | It cost a hundred | It has a hundred gears | It goes a hundred kilometres
test: Racing to the swings, a kid yells «ostANniy — loh!». You're last. What is it? | A friendly tease in fun | A real fight starting | A new rule you broke | The game being cancelled
test: In hide-and-seek the seeker counts to sto. Compared with counting to DEsyat, you have… | Ten times longer to hide | Exactly the same time to hide | Less time | Twice as long
test: «skIlky?» asks the stall lady pointing at the apples. What does she want to know? | How many you want | How old you are | Where you live | What colour they are
test: A girl crosses the line first and shouts «ya PERshyi!». What should she say? | ya PERsha! | ya PERshyi! is right | ya ostANnya | ya PERshe
test: «shut up, mEnshe means more» Which is right? | No: mEnshe is less, bIlshe is more | Yes, mEnshe is more, bIlshe is less | Both mean more | Both mean less
test: «raz, dva, try — pishLY!» What happens on pishLY? | Everyone starts running | Everyone hides behind a tree | Everyone stops | Everyone sits down
exam: State the numeral signified by devyanOsto, which is not formed in the regular manner from its unit. | Ninety | Nineteen | Nine hundred | Nine, the unit
exam: Identify the colour denoted in Ukrainian by chervOnyi, noting that krasNyi signifies something else. | Red; krasNyi signifies beautiful | Beautiful; krasNyi signifies red | Black; krasNyi signifies white | Orange; krasNyi signifies brown
exam: Ascertain the function of the utterance ya shukAyu! in the game known as khovAnky. | It is pronounced by the seeker upon completing the count, before searching | It is pronounced by a hidden player upon being found | It signals a draw | It signals that the game is cancelled
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning counting on the fingers. | Many Ukrainians bend the fingers into the palm one by one, so that a number may appear otherwise than in English counting | All nations display numbers upon the fingers in an identical manner, so that no difference whatever may be observed between Ukrainian and English counting | Ukrainians count exclusively with the toes | Counting on the fingers is unknown in Ukraine
